{"cuisine/aztec-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/aztec-cuisine","n":"Aztec cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Mesoamerican cuisine","cuisine/mesoamerican-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Mexico","intro":[["Today, Nahuatl-speaking ",["Nahua","cuisine/nahua-foodways"]," communities still farm the milpa and cook with corn, beans and chile."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Secretaría de Cultura and Colectivo Siwalcentli on Google Arts & Culture, Nahua Siwameh itech Tlekuille","url":"https://artsandculture.google.com/story/JgWhWhzqlzrcMQ"},{"name":"University of Texas at Austin, Tlahtolli, Our cornfield and our food","url":"https://tlahtolli.coerll.utexas.edu/tlamachtiliztli-20-lesson-20-nomillah-huah-notlacualiztli-our-cornfield-and-our-food/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"aztec-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aztec_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Mesoamerican cuisine","cuisine/mesoamerican-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Mesoamerican cuisine","cuisine/mesoamerican-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d9/Aztec_shared_meal.jpg/500px-Aztec_shared_meal.jpg","w":500,"h":406,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aztec_shared_meal.jpg"}},"cuisine/swedish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/swedish-cuisine","n":"Swedish cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Sweden","intro":[["Swedish cuisine is the traditional food of Sweden. Due to Sweden's large north-to-south expanse, there are regional differences between the cuisine of North and South Sweden."],["Historically, in the far north, meats such as reindeer, and other game dishes were eaten, some of which have their roots in the Sami culture, while fresh vegetables have played a larger role in the South. Many traditional dishes employ simple, contrasting flavours, such as the traditional dish of meatballs and brown cream sauce with tart, pungent lingonberry jam."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"swedish-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swedish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Scandicuisine: Swedish recipes","ref":"site/scandicuisine-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"scandicuisine.com","note":"Traditional Swedish recipes from Cecilia, a cook from a Finnish-Swedish family on Åland.","url":"https://scandicuisine.com/category/swedish-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"The Nordic Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/the-nordic-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Magnus Nilsson, 2015","note":"Swedish chef's large survey of home cooking across the Nordic countries, with strong Swedish coverage.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780714868721","suggested":true},{"name":"The Little Swedish Kitchen","ref":"cookbook/the-little-swedish-kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Rachel Khoo, 2015","note":"Swedish home recipes from the cook's time living in Stockholm.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780718188917","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":22,"links":[["Biff à la Lindström","dish/biff-a-la-lindstrom"],["Blåbärssoppa","dish/blabarssoppa"],["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Flying Jacob","dish/flying-jacob"],["Fruktsoppa","dish/fruktsoppa"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Hasselback potatoes","dish/hasselback-potatoes"],["Isterband","dish/isterband"],["Kebab pizza","dish/kebab-pizza"],["Korv stroganoff","dish/korv-stroganoff"],["Kroppkaka","dish/kroppkaka"],["Limpa","dish/limpa"],["Lutefisk","dish/lutefisk"],["Oatmeal ball","dish/oatmeal-ball"],["Ostkaka","dish/ostkaka"],["Rødgrød","dish/r-dgr-d"],["Semla","dish/semla"],["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"],["Strong beer","dish/strong-beer"],["Surströmming","dish/surstromming"],["Swedish meatballs","dish/swedish-meatballs"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Ground meat","ingredient/ground-meat"],["Potato starch","ingredient/potato-starch"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Beta vulgaris","ingredient/beta-vulgaris"],["Capparis","ingredient/capparis"],["Bilberries","ingredient/bilberries"],["Bilberry","ingredient/bilberry"],["Starch","ingredient/starch"],["Blood","ingredient/blood"],["Blood as food","ingredient/blood-as-food"],["Cabbage","ingredient/cabbage"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Banana","ingredient/banana"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili sauce","ingredient/chili-sauce"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/dd/H%C3%A4ssleholm_-_Midsommar_Dinner_%283703837461%29.jpg/500px-H%C3%A4ssleholm_-_Midsommar_Dinner_%283703837461%29.jpg","w":500,"h":315,"by":"Roger W from Sarasota, Florida, U.S.A.","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:H%C3%A4ssleholm_-_Midsommar_Dinner_(3703837461).jpg"}},"cuisine/ancient-greek-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/ancient-greek-cuisine","n":"Ancient Greek cuisine","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Historical cuisine","intro":[["Ancient Greek cuisine was characterized by its frugality for most, reflecting agricultural hardship, but a great diversity of ingredients was known, and wealthy Greeks were known to celebrate with elaborate meals and feasts."],["The cuisine was founded on the \"Mediterranean triad\" of cereals, olives, and grapes, which had many uses and great commercial value, but other ingredients were as important, if not more so, to the average diet: most notably legumes. Research suggests that the agricultural system of ancient Greece could not have succeeded without the cultivation of legumes."],["Modern knowledge of ancient Greek cuisine and eating habits is derived from textual, archeological, and artistic evidence."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ancient-greek-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Greek_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Classical Cookbook (revised edition)","ref":"cookbook/the-classical-cookbook-revised-edition","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Andrew Dalby and Sally Grainger, 2012","note":"Getty Publications book of Greek and Roman recipes adapted from ancient texts by two food historians.","url":"https://shop.getty.edu/products/the-classical-cookbook-revised-edition-978-1606061107","suggested":true}]}],"cont":null},"ingredient/platostoma-palustre":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/platostoma-palustre","n":"Platostoma palustre","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Platostoma palustre, commonly known as Chinese mesona, is a species of plant belonging to the genus Platostoma of the mint family. The species grows extensively in East Asia such as south east China, Japan and Taiwan preferring ravines, grassy, dry, and sandy areas. The plants are from 15 to 100 cm high with hairy stems and leaves. The leaves are tear-drop shaped and serrated."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-platostoma-palustre"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Platostoma_palustre"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Es doger","dish/es-doger"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Avocado","ingredient/avocado"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Ice","ingredient/ice"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Syrup","ingredient/syrup"],["Tapioca pearl","ingredient/tapioca-pearl"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/93/Grass_jelly_cultivation_in_Singapore.jpg/500px-Grass_jelly_cultivation_in_Singapore.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Ronggy","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grass_jelly_cultivation_in_Singapore.jpg"}},"ingredient/ribes":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/ribes","n":"Ribes","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Ribes ( RY-beez) is a genus of approximately 200 known species of flowering plants, predominantly native to the temperate regions of the Northern Hemisphere. Ribes is the sole genus in the plant family Grossulariaceae. The genus includes species commonly referred to as redcurrants, blackcurrants, whitecurrants, and gooseberries. Several species are cultivated both for their edible fruit and as ornamental plants in horticulture. Ribes are the main alternate host for white pine blister rust, a fungal pathogen impacting five-needle pines."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-ribes"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribes"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Flies' graveyard","dish/flies-graveyard"]]],["Dutch cuisine","cuisine/dutch-cuisine",[["Oliebol","dish/oliebol"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Blackcurrant","ingredient/blackcurrant"],["Baking powder","ingredient/baking-powder"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Succade","ingredient/succade"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Sultana (grape)","ingredient/sultana-grape"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Zest","ingredient/zest"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4c/Ribes_divaricatum_5391.JPG/500px-Ribes_divaricatum_5391.JPG","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Walter Siegmund (talk)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ribes_divaricatum_5391.JPG"}},"dish/kasha":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/kasha","n":"Kasha","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 6 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["In English, kasha () is a porridge usually made from buckwheat, a pseudocereal. In the Slavic languages, kasha means porridge. In some varieties of Central and Eastern European cuisine, kasha can apply to any kind of cooked grain. It can be baked but most often is boiled, either in water or milk, but the word can also refer to the grain before preparation, which corresponds to the definition of 'groats'."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-kasha"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kasha"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine"],["Lithuanian cuisine","cuisine/lithuanian-cuisine"],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"],["Slovenian cuisine","cuisine/slovenian-cuisine"],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Barley","ingredient/barley"],["Buckwheat","ingredient/buckwheat"],["Millet","ingredient/millet"],["Oats","ingredient/oats"],["Rye","ingredient/rye"],["Wheat","ingredient/wheat"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Polonist","ref":"site/polonist-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"polonist.com","note":"Polish recipes and food culture from a Polish-born writer living abroad.","url":"https://www.polonist.com/about/","suggested":true},{"name":"Polish Your Kitchen","ref":"site/polishyourkitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"polishyourkitchen.com","note":"Long-running blog of traditiona